var e=`<p>Open and distance learning and online learning are frequently used as though they meant the same thing. They are separate modes, regulated separately, and they produce a noticeably different experience.</p>
<h2>Open and distance learning</h2>
<p>The older model. The university provides study material, printed or digital, you work through it largely on your own, and there are periodic contact sessions at a study centre. Assessment is usually a written examination at the end of each semester, often at a physical centre, sometimes with assignments contributing.</p>
<p>It suits people with limited or unreliable internet, people who prefer reading material to watching lectures, and people who are comfortable with a single concentrated examination rather than continuous deadlines.</p>
<h2>Online learning</h2>
<p>Delivered through a learning platform: recorded or live lectures, digital material, discussion forums, and assessment through a mix of continuous assignments, quizzes and remotely proctored examinations.</p>
<p>It suits people with reliable connectivity, people who want to study in short blocks across the week, and people whose schedule will not survive travelling to a centre for examinations.</p>
<h2>The differences that actually matter</h2>
<ul>
<li><p>Where you sit examinations. Centre-based examinations mean travel on fixed dates. Remote proctoring means a laptop, a camera and a quiet room. For shift workers and people who travel, this is often the whole decision.</p>
</li>
<li><p>How the workload is spread. Continuous assessment spreads it evenly and punishes a bad month. A single end-of-semester examination concentrates it and punishes a bad fortnight.</p>
</li>
<li><p>How much structure you get. Online programmes with live sessions give you a timetable. Distance programmes give you material and a date, and the pacing is yours.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Connectivity. An online programme with unreliable internet is a genuinely bad experience, and no amount of enthusiasm fixes it.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2>What is the same</h2>
<p>Both require the university to hold entitlement for that programme in that mode. Both produce a degree treated as equivalent to a campus one when that entitlement is in place. Neither is a lesser qualification.</p>
<h2>Choosing</h2>
<p>Answer three questions honestly. Can you reach an examination centre on a fixed date. Is your internet reliable enough for proctored assessment. Do you work better against frequent small deadlines or one large one.</p>
<p>Those three answers usually decide it without any further comparison. Tell us your answers and we will tell you which of our partner universities&#39; programmes match, and confirm the entitlement before you pay anything.</p>
`;export{e as html};